Every clinician should evaluate a risk for an opioid-related-harm before an opioid-initiation.
Every clinician should periodically evaluate a risk for an opioid-related-harm during a continued-opioid-therapy.
If a clinician uses an opioid-therapy for a patient then the clinician should periodically discuss a risk for an opioid-related-harm with the patient.
If a risk-mitigation-strategy is relevant for a patient and a clinician uses an opioid-therapy for the patient then the clinician should periodically incorporate the risk-mitigation-strategy in a management-plan with the patient.
If a clinician uses an opioid-therapy for a patient and the patient takes a prescribed-medication then the clinician should periodically review a potential-interaction with the prescribed-medication for the patient.
If a clinician uses an opioid-therapy for a patient and the patient uses a substance then the clinician should periodically review a potential-interaction with the substance for the patient.
If a risk-mitigation-strategy is relevant for a patient and a clinician considers an opioid-initiation for the patient then the clinician should incorporate the risk-mitigation-strategy in a management-plan with the patient before the opioid-initiation.
If a clinician considers an opioid-initiation for a patient then the clinician should offer a naloxone-dose to the patient before the opioid-initiation.
If a clinician uses an opioid-therapy for a patient then the clinician should periodically offer a naloxone-dose to the patient.
If a clinician considers an opioid-initiation for a patient and the patient takes a prescribed-medication then the clinician should review a potential-interaction with the prescribed-medication for the patient before the opioid-initiation.
If a clinician considers an opioid-initiation for a patient and the patient uses a substance then the clinician should review a potential-interaction with the substance for the patient before the opioid-initiation.
